you can finally experience destiny 2 on  PC with the beta that hit our gaming  rigs but do you know how your system's  gonna handle it  establish fans of the franchise and  those who wanted the game to come to PC  should be hyped up the game runs  extremely well even on Modest systems  I'm running an Intel Core i5 35 70 K CPU  an MSI GTX 970 slightly overclocked and  8 gigabytes of ddr3 RAM which falls  pretty much in line with what Bungie and  Activision States as the recommended  system specs for destiny 2 but what does  that translate to what could we actually  get out of these specs but before we  move on make sure you're subscribed to  GameSpot for more game tech first let's  take a look at our options in the  graphics menu Bungie didn't skimp out on  the PC version as promised we have the  option for an uncapped framerate there's  also an option to lock it at 60 FPS or  even 30 FPS but there really isn't any  reason to do this one of the most  important and sometimes overlooked  options and shooters is field of view or  FOV it's essentially how wide your  viewing angle is of the game environment  the higher it is the more you can see in  your peripheral vision it's a  competitive advantage since you'll see  more of what's going on at any given  moment so crank that coveted fov slider  up somewhere between 95 and 105 though  personally I go with the max at 105 now  let's dig into actual visual effects to  get rid of jaggies we effectively have 4  anti-aliasing options FXAA SMA a MSAA  and super sampling fxaa tends to make  the game look a little too blurry so I'd  opt for SMA since it strikes a nice  balance between performance and quality  while the better-looking MSA 8ax my  machine too much with all our settings  cranked up at 1080p our quick little  test in the opening area showed a frame  rate around the high 60s to the high 70s  with SMA 8 with msaa the frame rate  dipped around the low to mid 40s it's  not worth the 30 or so FPS trade-off  with my modest system but note that this  is still a beta and Bungie said that  MSAA is still being optimized resolution  scale is synonymous with super sampling  you force your machine to produce a  higher resolution image and downscale it  to fit your displays proper resolution  it's the most effective form of  anti-aliasing but it's also the most  demanding at 133 percent  essentially 1440p the framerate dipped  to about the mid-40s  another big hitter in the graphic  settings is ambient occlusion which is a  process of darkening objects that should  be blocked by light sources creating a  sort of fake shadow that adds richness  and depth to the scene there are two  options HD AO and 3d as for performance  with everything maxed out including MSAA  the difference between HD AO and 3d was  around 5 frames per second in the same  test scene  run clear as to exactly what the 3d  setting is but if I had to guess it's  something similar to the advance of VX  AO  I saw a few more objects in the game  rolled with better shadowing but I felt  I had to really look hard for it so I  opted for the extra fps with HD AO when  we turn ambient occlusion completely off  we got three to five extra fps but the  overall image looked flatter so I find  HD AO worth it anisotropic filtering is  labeled as texture and I sought repeat  this setting cleans up the quality of  textures off in the distance and  surfaces that are seen at an oblique  angle it's one of the more visually  impactful effects that doesn't ask too  much from your hardware  I'd suggest cranking this up to 16 X  since the performance hit if any is  negligible it's like a free bump  individual quality many of the other  settings are quite self-explanatory such  as quality of textures shadows or the  distance at which certain things like  character models and foliage keep their  quality chromatic aberration and film  grain don't really have a performance  impact and are more about personal  preference when it was all set and done  I settled with 1080 P SMA a HD AO 16 x  texture filtering and all the quality  settings set to the highest through my  several hours in multiplayer matches the  game ranked consistently above 60 frames  per second bouncing between the mid 70s  to the mid 90s only in the most frantic  moments did I notice the framerate dip  to the high 50s but that's negligible  you shouldn't expect to run the game at  4k at the recommended specs but we gave  it a shot anyway you turn off  anti-aliasing use HD AO turn all the  quality settings to high but shadows to  medium the results were between the mid  20s to the mid 30s which is undesirable  given that it's a disadvantage for a  competitive PC game where everyone else  is likely getting upwards of 60 frames  per second 4k is reserved for those  equipped with the GTX 970 or an Rx Vega  56 at the very release but you probably  want a GTX 1080 1080 Ti or arts Vega 64  new 4k comfortably overall the pc  version of destiny 2 is shaping up to be  a great version of the game and runs  fast with high graphics settings even on  modest hardware on top of that Bungie is  giving us an uncapped frame rate and fov  slider in even a built in FPS counter  keep in mind that this is still a beta  options we see here could change by the  time of the final release and future  drivers and updates can improve  performance metrics there's so much more  I could talk about with graphics  settings alone but I want to throw the  discussion to you are you excited for  destiny to which platform will you be  playing on if you want a deeper dive  into the options we talked about here  check out our explainer videos on PC  graphics settings like anti-aliasing or  ambient occlusion subscribe for more  stuff like this and like this video if  you found it insightful thanks for  watching we'll see you next time
 

